The baseline already uses the minimum work for this computation: one plaintext multiply and one rotation, at one level. The rotation is unavoidable because output slot i needs input slot i+offset, and the mask is a plaintext diagonal that must be applied elementwise. Rotating after the multiply keeps the rotation at the already-reduced level, which is cheaper than rotating the fresh ciphertext first. Rotating the mask plaintext instead would not reduce the ciphertext operations, so I keep the baseline structure and make the offset explicit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  // One ct-pt multiply (consumes one level), then one rotation applied at the
  // reduced level after rescale. This is the minimal op count for this kernel.
  Ciphertext prod = input * mask;
  Ciphertext output = prod << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

The last attempt performed the same operations as the baseline, and its 60 ms is within noise of the 59 ms baseline. To test a different structure, this attempt rotates the input first and multiplies by the pre-rotated mask. That moves the rotation to the fresh ciphertext and the multiply to the rotated ciphertext, so the op count is unchanged but the cost distribution differs. It may be slower, since the rotation then runs at the full level.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  // Alternative ordering: rotate the input first, then multiply by the
  // rotated mask so that the product lands in the same slots as before.
  Ciphertext rotated = input << offset;
  Ciphertext output = rotated * (mask << offset);
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

The failure came from rotating the plaintext mask: `mask << offset` is a plaintext-only expression, which the backend cannot evaluate. The fix is to keep every rotation on a ciphertext, which means reverting to the baseline ordering (multiply, then rotate). That ordering is already the best measured so far at 59.11 ms, so I'm returning to it.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext input("input");
  Plaintext mask("mask");
  const int offset = static_cast<int>(slots() / 4);
  // Multiply by the mask first, then rotate the (already reduced-level) product.
  // Plaintext rotations are not allowed, so the rotation stays on the ciphertext.
  Ciphertext output = (input * mask) << offset;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I'm stopping the search here and returning the baseline kernel as the final answer. Every variant I tried has the same operation count (one ct-pt multiply, one rotation, one rescale) and timings land between 59 and 63 ms, so the differences are noise. The only structural alternative, rotating before multiplying, was rejected by the backend. I don't see a cheaper formulation that avoids the rotation, since output slot i must read input slot i+offset.

